{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `noopGuardHeadIsRed` fails OPEN on a degraded (non-throwing) check-runs read**

The documented invariant (block comment above) is: *"An UNREADABLE rollup (null) still fails closed to red: suppression demands positive no-red evidence."* In the implementation that invariant only holds when the `github_get_commit_check_runs` tool **throws**. When it returns an unparseable payload (HTTP error body, empty string, missing `check_runs` key), the chain is:

```javascript
// headCheckRunsSafe: mcpParse(raw) -> null on JSON.parse failure, then:
var cr = mcpParse(raw) || {};
return cr.check_runs || cr.checkRuns || [];   // degraded read becomes []
```

so `noopGuardHeadIsRed` receives `[]` — indistinguishable from a genuine "CI never ran on this head" — and returns `false` (no red). The suppression then needs only a positively-empty thread census to fire.

Concretely: head actually red **+** GraphQL census reads 0 **+** REST check-runs payload unparseable-but-not-throwing → escalation suppressed on degraded evidence. Damage is bounded downstream (validation re-dispatch → `validation_failed` park → `rework-on-validation-failed` re-arm, plus `redHeadSkip`), so this is not a queue-wedge, but it contradicts the code's own stated fail-closed contract.

Suggested fix — make the red probe parse-aware instead of reusing the collapse-to-`[]` wrapper, e.g. a variant that returns `null` when the payload did not parse / has no `check_runs` array:

```javascript
var raw = github_get_commit_check_runs({...});
var cr = mcpParse(raw);
if (!cr || (!cr.check_runs && !cr.checkRuns)) return true; // degraded -> fail closed
var rollup = cr.check_runs || cr.checkRuns;
```

(a genuine empty `check_runs: []` still reads as no-red, preserving the deliberate empty-rollup semantics).
